A||||7||Formula||3.1 liter motor question (IMPORTANT)||||1||
Z||000000||Formula||08-16-2000||09:52 AM||||If I switch over to the 3.1 out of a minivan from the early 90s would I get better gas mileage... or would I need the tranny too?<P>and what GM minivan used the 3.1 V6... I need to find a way to get better gas mileage or I have to say bye bye to the fiero.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Formula_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Xtreme Fiero's||08-16-2000||10:12 AM||||have you thought about a quad-4? from what I have heard it's performance is about that of a stock fiero 2.8, and I would think that since it as a 2.2/2.4L 4-cyl it would get better gas mileage than a 2.8, but I don't know for sure I asume you have a 2.8 going by your name & sig. <P>I think you could get a 3.1L V6 from one of thoes Pontiac mini vans i forget the name but they have the wierd tail lights that run up the top corners. but for fuel economy my moms buick regal custom has a 3.4 that gets 20mpg draging around a 3000lb car w/ an automatic. and I think all you need to do to a 3.4 is move the starter to the other side and you can even use your Fiero intake I think. Can any one confirm this.<BR> Hope I helped out a bit||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||08-16-2000||12:42 PM||||Gas milage is largly dependent on driving habits and transmissions and engine tuning. With my 2.8- 5 speed GT I can easily get 30 mpg over the road driving with cruise (mostly) at 70 mpg.  Around town it drops to mid 20 mpg.  But I drive easily. Not doing street drags.  With my 88 GT with a 3.1 and automatic I was lucky to get 26 mpg over the road under the same conditions and usually less than 20 around town.  The difference was bigger engine displacement and an automatic transmission that ran at 3200 rpm at 70 vs a 5th gear manual transmission running at 2400 rpm at 70.  The transmission from the minivan is probably a 4 speed automatic with a lockup, which will give you an overdrive and lower rpms which should give you better gas milage over the road.  There is the hassel of the engine swap and or engine and transmission swap.<P>If your current engine is well tuned up and all the components are working as they should I don't think you will gain much in improved milage with just a 3.1 engine swap except more power.  An engine and transmission both swapped out maybe better economy.<BR>Just how bad is your milage?||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||08-16-2000||12:50 PM||||Ok, so get this. Your mileage may vary.<P>I own a 3.1L TBI Trans Sport van. 200k miles. I regularly get 27 MPG cruising at 70MPH. The van weighs just shy of 4000lbs. It's essentially an A-car chassis. Can't get out of it own way. 0-60 must be 13 seconds.<P>Why, I don't know. I know it's not running lean. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||triker||08-16-2000||12:57 PM||||What kind of milage are you getting?  I've always gotten about 27 in my 87GT w/auto on the hwy.  If you're getting much less than that, you have correctable probems.  How much gas can you buy for the cost of a conversion?  Can you keep your foot out of it after you have that extra horse power?||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||08-16-2000||01:12 PM||||I believe most of the numbers others have posted.  They sound about right. My (well-worn) 1987 Fiero GT 5-speed gets 30-32 mpg steady-state highway; 20-22-ish in town (no playing around).  My 1987 Cavalier with a 1990 3.1 V6 and Getrag gets more like 26 highway.<P>Think you might do well fixing up the existing V6, rather than going for an engine swap.  Of course, there is one short-term benefit - while the car is down for the duration of the swap, it won't be burning any gas  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !  ||||reg||6
Z||000006||Formula||08-17-2000||12:18 PM||||I get about 20 mpg on the highway at 65-70 mph.  the motor has around 70-80,000 miles on it and needs replaced anyways, the transmission has 150k on it and is still working fine but I think it should be replaced. everything elses on the car has been replaced except for a couple sensors and the egr was removed at one point and the "person" blocked it off properly.  I'm leaking oil out of the distributor o-ring so I think I might as well just replace the whole thing for $100 and it might pick up some mpg (I dunno though).  I'm hoping to find a 3.1 and tranny out of a minivan before college starts so that I can get around 30 mpg at around 65 mph..... right now I have to fill up every morning and it costs me around $10......10x365....so if I could find the engine tranny combo for a grand or 2 with around 30k on it then I  should be a little better off....<P>if I do go to this engine can I use my new alternator and water pump?  and will I need the computer out of the minivan too?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Formula_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||pherder||08-17-2000||06:46 PM||||I "tried" one of those 3.1 cast iron APV van engines.  Mine was out of a 93 Chevy Lumina APV.<P>I swapped the Fiero outer "stuff" to the 3.1 block and installed it into my 88 GT.<P>Coolant puked out somewhere by the timing cover and the lower hose.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>E-mailed Ed Parks and he told me he remembers having to weld up a hole in the timing cover that is just under the water pump in the cavity of the lower hose.  Also the block has a boss that has to be plugged and he found that he could only have it welded otherwise it would leak.<P>Well, no matter what I do, it leaks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>So I am putting the 3.1 crank and rods along with a set of new oversize 3.1 pistons into a stock Fiero 2.8 block and I hope to stop this @!$!@ coolant leak.<P>IF you get one of those 3.1 engines ... good luck!<P>Paul||||reg||11
